UNIT-III
Understand: How to make Formal Presentations. Practice: Formal

Presentations

4.3. MAKING A PRESENTAION

OBJECTIVE:
To be able to prepare presentations and present them in front of an audience.
SKILLS DEVELOPED:
a. How to plan a presentation.
b. How to present it to an audience.
c. How to respond to questions.
RUBRIC FOR ASSESSMENT:
a. Relevance and complexity of topic(appropriate to level of students).
b. Content of presentation.
c. Manner of presentation, body language, intonation.
d. Handling of audience responses.
Time limit: 25 minutes per presentation.
Planning: 10 minutes.
Preparation of slides/charts: 10 minutes.
Presentation: 5 minutes.
Mode: Group work.
Presentations can be made in different ways:
 Computer projection (using application such as Excel, PowerPoint, etc.)
 Flipcharts, blackboards or whiteboards, sometimes used as ‘scratchpads’ to expand on a
point.
 Video or film.
 Real objects (such as a product or plan specimen).
PREPARING A PRESENTATION:

 Decide the subject of the presentation. If it has not been assigned to you , choose a topic
that suits the occasion and the audience.
 Think about the objective or purpose: is it meant to inform, persuade or report?
 Think of who your audience is going to be; the level of your content, formality and style
will depend on this.
 Next, list the main points you want to make in your presentation. It is important not to
pack too much content into a presentation because this will diffuse its effect. For
example, a presentation of about twenty minutes should not have more than five main
points. After you have a final list of points, arrange them in a logical sequence.
 Think of sub-points that could come under your main points. It is best to put only the
main points and the sub-points on slide (or flip chart) and speak briefly on each of these.
 Do not put in too much text into the slide. This makes the slide crowded and also difficult
to read.
 Prepare graphs, tables or pie charts that you want to show on the slides.
 Ensure that the presentation has a clear conclusion.
 Plan your presentation in a way that will enable you to the time allotted to you.
 Finally, prepare your presentation early and rehearse it as many times as you can.
STRUCTURING CONTENT:
 The first part must consist of greetings, a brief, clear statement of the subject and purpose
of the presentation and an outline.
 The second part must have the main content of the presentation.
 The last part must have a brief recap or a summary of the most important points.
VISUAL AIDS:
 All the visuals you show must be related to your talk.
 Make sure the slides, charts or transparencies are in the right order, matching the
sequence of point in your presentation.
 Do not put in too much information on one visual.
 Written matter on slides and transparencies should be in the form of key words or
phrases, points or short sentences.
 Prepare your slides in such a way that the audience sees only one visual or point at a
time.
 It is useful to give the audience handouts at the end of your presentation.
 Visuals should be simple, clear and appealing.
 Use a minimum 20-point Times Roman or any other friendly typeface that can be read
from a distance.

DELIVERING THE PRESENTATION:
 Stick to your original plan for the presentation. Do not take up other matters, however
interesting they may be , because this will lead to confusion.
 Modulate your voice according to the size of the room and the audience.
 Before you show a slide or a transparency, tell the audience something about it. After it is
put on screen, explain it more fully.
 Do not read out the matter on the screen or a prepared script.
 Do not block the screen or stand facing it so that your back is turned to the audience.
 Remember that your appearance, posture, facial expressions, eye contact with the
audience and body language are important non-verbal signal.
OPENING REMARKS:
 Hello, everyone. (appropriate for an informal presentation for a small group of people
you interact with every day)
 Good morning/Good afternoon/Good evening. Welcome to...(name of
organization)/Thank you for giving me the opportunity to talk to you today.
 Good morning. I’d like to/I’m happy to welcome you all here today.
STATING THE PURPOSE:
 As you know, the subject of my presentation today is...
 I’m here this morning to...
 My aim is to...
GIVING AN OUTLINE:
 I’ve divided my presentation into...
 I’ll first..., then we’ll... Finally, I’ll...
 I’ll begin by... and after that I’ll deal with... before going on to... The presentation will
conclude with...
GIVING OTHER PRELIMINARY INFORMATION AND STARTING WITH THE
CONTENT:
 My presentation will take about half an hour or so. If you don’t mind, could I deal with
questions after the talk, please.
 I’ll take only about fifteen minutes of your time. We’ll have a question-and-answer
session after that.
 The presentation is going to take around forty minutes. Please feel free to interrupt if you
have a question.

 You don’t need to/needn’t take notes. I’ll give a set of handouts with all the points we
discuss today.
MOVING TO ANSWER POINT OR GOING BACK TO AN EARLIER POINT:
 Let’s now turn to...
 This takes me to my next point about...
 Let me go back briefly to an earlier point.
EMPHASISING IMPORTANT POINTS:
 What we must understand/realize/do is...
 What we mustn’t do is...
 We urgently/really need to...
DRAWING ATTENTION TO VISUALS:
 I’d like to draw your attention to...
 Could you just look at the... on the screen.
 As you’ll see in the next slide.
MAKING RECOMMENDATIONS:
 I (strongly) recommend that...
 My recommendation is that...
 I really think/believe we should...
KEEPING THE AUDIENCE INVOLVED:
 How would you solve this problem?
 Where are we heading?
 Can you think of a way of dealing with this?
 What are the options open to us?
 Why should we be concerned about this?
SUMMARISING AND CONCLUDING:
 To sum up the main points of my presentation,...
 Before I end my talk, I’d like to summaries its main points.
 To run through/recap my main points,...
INVITING QUESTIONS:
 If you have any questions, please feel free to ask them.
 If want to ask any questions, I’ll do my best to answer them.
 If there are any questions, I’ll be pleased to answer them.